yield: fills a 500ml jar
components:
  - name: brine
    ingredients:
      - item: apple cider vinegar
        mass: 150
        note: sub white wine vinegar; rice vinegar for a softer, sweeter pickle
      - item: water
        mass: 150
      - item: sugar
        mass: 30
      - item: salt
        mass: 10
      - item: black peppercorns
        qty: 1
        unit: tsp
        optional: true
      - item: bay leaf
        qty: 1
        unit: ea
        optional: true
    steps:
      - heat everything until sugar and salt just dissolve, don't boil
  - name: red onion
    ingredients:
      - item: red onion
        mass: 200
        qty: 1
        unit: ea
        basis: true
        note: large, sliced 2mm on a mandoline
    steps:
      - slice thin, pack into the jar
      - pour over hot brine to cover
      - 10 mins to edible, 30 mins to properly pink, best after 2 hrs
      - keeps 3 weeks in the fridge
  - name: cucumber
    ingredients:
      - item: lebanese cucumber
        mass: 200
        note: 3mm rounds
      - item: dill
        optional: true
        note: few sprigs
      - item: garlic
        qty: 1
        unit: clove
        note: smashed
        optional: true
    steps:
      - salt the rounds 15 mins and pat dry first, or they go soft
      - pack with dill and garlic, pour over brine cooled to warm — hot brine cooks the crunch out
      - 1 hr minimum
      - keeps 1 week in the fridge, crunch fades after 3-4 days
notes: |
  Not a preserve — no canning, no shelf stability, straight into the fridge.

  Hot brine for onion (you want it softened and fully pink), warm brine for cucumber (you
  want it to stay crunchy). That's the only real difference between the two.

  On a pulled pork sandwich the pickles are doing a different job to the slaw: the slaw is
  bulk crunch and creamy acid across the whole bite, the pickles are sharp punctuation.
  Both, not either.
